Rem ===================================================================
Rem mungo-caverns-state.shoddy -- the game record, and the questions asked of it.
Rem 
Rem The game record: one record threaded through every turn,
Rem plus the small predicates -- Here, At, Toting, IsDarkHere -- that
Rem the rules ask about it constantly.
Rem ===================================================================

Rem ---- state ---------------------------------------------------------------
Rem One record threads the whole game through every call. Two fields need
Rem explaining. ObjectsAt is an array of per-room object lists, because the
Rem order objects are listed in is observable: dropping prefixes to the
Rem room's list. Held is the same list
Rem for the player's hands.

Def Carried() As Number
    0 - 1

Rem Suspended is not Quit: a suspended game ends the moment the file is
Rem written, so it is never scored and never rated.
Rem Quitting, dying and running out of input all still go past the scorer.
Type Fate = Playing | Quit | Died(Msg) | Suspended | Won

Type Game
    Location             As Number
    PreviousLocation     As Number
    ObjectsAt            As Array
    Held                 As List Of Number
    ObjectLocation       As Array
    Props                As Array
    PendingObject        As Number
    PendingVerb          As Number
    PendingRaw           As String
    LocationSeen         As Array
    Turns                As Number
    Deaths               As Number
    Tally                As Number
    CurmudgeonActivation As Number
    Curmudgeons          As List
    KnifeLocation        As Number
    LocationBeforeLast   As Number
    LeftRoomWasDark      As Boolean
    FooBarProgress       As String
    SeenBigWords         As Boolean
    WestCount            As Number
    GoCount              As Number
    TurnPenalty          As Number
    LampWarned           As Boolean
    Blooded              As Boolean
    CurmudgeonsKilled    As Number
    ClockToClosing       As Number
    ClockToBlinding      As Number
    ClosingTime          As Boolean
    Closed               As Boolean
    Panic                As Boolean
    Bonus                As Number
    ReadEndgameClue      As Boolean
    SavePenalty          As Number
    Detail               As Number
    AbbrevInterval       As Number
    HintState            As List
    PreviousObject       As Number
    Fixture              As Array
    LcgState             As Number
    MagicWord            As String
    Novice               As Boolean
    LampLife             As Number
    Outcome              As Fate
    Rem What the rules have said so far this turn. See mungo-caverns-say.shoddy:
    Rem rules append here instead of printing, and the turn loop drains it.
    Rem WriteSaveAs enumerates fields explicitly, so this one is simply not
    Rem written and the save format is unchanged.
    Output               As List Of String


Def NoObject() As Number
    0

Rem Verb 0 (ACT_NULL) means "no verb remembered yet".
Def NoVerb() As Number
    0

Def VerbWord(g As Game) As String
    PendingRaw(g)

Rem The room lists must land in declaration order, with the two-placed
Rem objects described last. Two forward appending passes -- singles then
Rem doubles -- produce exactly that.
Def PlacedTwice(i As Number, doubles As Boolean) As Boolean
    Let ps = Places(Nth(objects, i))
    (Length(ps) > 1) = doubles And Not IsEmpty(ps)

Def SeedPass(at As Array, n As Number, doubles As Boolean) As Array
    Range(1, n)
        Filter(Fn(i) => PlacedTwice(i, doubles))
        Fold(at, Fn(acc, i) => PlaceAll(acc, Places(Nth(objects, i)), i - 1))

Def SeedRooms(at As Array, n As Number) As Array
    SeedPass(SeedPass(at, n, False), n, True)

Rem A few objects exist in two rooms at once -- the grate seen from above
Rem and below, the troll at both ends of its bridge. Listing the object
Rem in both rooms models the second placement without index arithmetic.
Def PlaceAll(at As Array, ps As List, obj As Number) As Array
    Fold(ps, at, Fn(acc, loc) => SetNth(acc, loc + 1, Append(Nth(acc, loc + 1), obj)))

Def SeedWhere(wh As Array, n As Number) As Array
    Range(1, n)
        Filter(Fn(i) => Not IsEmpty(Places(Nth(objects, i))))
        Fold(wh, Fn(acc, i) => SetNth(acc, i, First(Places(Nth(objects, i)))))

Def EmptyLists(a As Array, n As Number) As Array
    Range(1, n)
        Fold(a, Fn(acc, i) => SetNth(acc, i, { }))

Rem A treasure that can be carried starts out "not found",
Rem which is the negative prop the rules lean on so heavily; everything else
Rem starts at zero. Describing a treasure is what flips it and decrements
Rem the tally, and the tally reaching zero is what closes the cave.
Def IsTreasure(o As Obj) As Boolean
    Treasure(o) And Len(Inventory(o)) > 0

Def SeedProps(p As Array, n As Number) As Array
    Range(1, n)
        Filter(Fn(i) => IsTreasure(Nth(objects, i)))
        Fold(p, Fn(acc, i) => SetNth(acc, i, 0 - 1))

Def TreasureCount() As Number
    Range(1, Length(objects) - 1)
        CountIf(Fn(i) => Treasure(ObjAt(i)))

Def NewGame(seed As Number) As Game
    Let nl = Length(locations)
    Let no = Length(objects)
    Let at = EmptyLists(Dim(nl, 0), nl)
    Let wh = Dim(no, 0)
    Rem PreviousLocation deliberately starts at LOC_NOWHERE -- which is
    Rem why "back" on turn one has nowhere to go.
    Rem Named rather than positional, and not as a matter of taste: this
    Rem record has forty-four fields, and given positionally, inserting one
    Rem in the middle silently shifts every argument after it into the wrong
    Rem field. Nothing would fail to compile.
    Game(Location             = 1,
         PreviousLocation     = 0,
         LocationBeforeLast   = 1,
         ObjectsAt            = SeedRooms(at, no),
         Held                 = { },
         ObjectLocation       = SeedWhere(wh, no),
         Props                = SeedProps(Dim(no, 0), no),
         Fixture              = SeedFixture(Dim(no, 0), no),
         LocationSeen         = Dim(nl, 0),

         PendingObject        = NoObject(),
         PendingVerb          = NoVerb(),
         PendingRaw           = "",
         PreviousObject       = NoObject(),

         Turns                = 0,
         Deaths               = 0,
         Tally                = TreasureCount(),
         TurnPenalty          = 0,
         SavePenalty          = 0,
         Bonus                = 0,

         CurmudgeonActivation = 0,
         Curmudgeons          = NewCurmudgeons(),
         CurmudgeonsKilled    = 0,
         KnifeLocation        = 0,

         ClockToClosing       = 30,
         ClockToBlinding      = 50,
         ClosingTime          = False,
         Closed               = False,
         Panic                = False,

         LampLife             = 330,
         LampWarned           = False,
         LeftRoomWasDark      = False,

         FooBarProgress       = "",
         SeenBigWords         = False,
         Blooded              = False,
         ReadEndgameClue      = False,
         WestCount            = 0,
         GoCount              = 0,

         HintState            = NewHints(),
         Detail               = 0,
         AbbrevInterval       = 5,
         Novice               = False,

         LcgState             = SeedState(seed),
         MagicWord            = ZzWord(seed),
         Outcome              = Playing(),
         Output               = Silent())

Def IsPlaying(g As Game) As Boolean
    Select Case Outcome(g)
        Case Playing
            True
        Case Else
            False

Def IsSuspended(g As Game) As Boolean
    Select Case Outcome(g)
        Case Suspended
            True
        Case Else
            False

Def RoomAt(g As Game) As Room
    Nth(locations, Location(g) + 1)

Def ObjAt(i As Number) As Obj
    Nth(objects, i + 1)

Def HereList(g As Game) As List Of Number
    Nth(ObjectsAt(g), Location(g) + 1)

Def Toting(g As Game, o As Number) As Boolean
    Contains(Held(g), o)

Rem ---- the generator -------------------------------------------------------

Def RollOf(g As Game, n As Number) As Number
    RandRange(LcgState(g), n)

Def Stepped(g As Game) As Game
    Trace("draw", Location(g), LcgState(g), RandRange(LcgState(g), 100))
    With(g, LcgState = LcgNext(LcgState(g)))

Rem ---- objects by name -------------------------------------------------------
Rem A handful of objects are special-cased by the rules, by name.

Rem The names themselves, and the three constants the rules ask for by
Rem name, live in mungo-caverns-names.shoddy. What is left here is the meaning of
Rem the prop values those two objects carry.

Def LampDark() As Number
    0

Def LampBright() As Number
    1

Def GrateClosed() As Number
    0

Def GrateOpen() As Number
    1

Def PropOf(g As Game, i As Number) As Number
    Nth(Props(g), i + 1)

Rem An object's prop is which state it is in -- except that a
Rem negative one means the state is there but hidden: the thing has not
Rem been laid eyes on yet, or the closing sequence stashed it into a
Rem repository pile so it stays undescribed until picked up and put down.
Rem
Rem The hiding is done with 0 - 1 - n, which is its own inverse, so the
Rem same expression stashes and unstashes. That is load-bearing and is not
Rem to be "simplified" into a negation: the round trip relies on it. Reading
Rem the encoding through this type is what keeps the arithmetic in one
Rem place rather than at every site that asks whether a thing is hidden.
Rem
Rem Note there is no separate "never found" case, tempting though it is to
Rem write one: a treasure starts life at 0 - 1, which is precisely Stashed
Rem at state zero. The two are the same value and nothing can tell them
Rem apart.
Type PropState = Stashed(State As Number) | InState(State As Number)

Def PropView(g As Game, i As Number) As PropState
    Let p = PropOf(g, i)
    If p < 0 Then
        Stashed(0 - 1 - p)
    Else
        InState(p)

Def PropNumber(s As PropState) As Number
    Select Case s
        Case Stashed(n)
            0 - 1 - n
        Case InState(n)
            n

Def SetPropView(g As Game, i As Number, s As PropState) As Game
    With(g, Props = SetNth(Props(g), i + 1, PropNumber(s)))

Rem "hidden" is the question nearly every caller is really asking.
Def IsHidden(g As Game, i As Number) As Boolean
    Select Case PropView(g, i)
        Case Stashed(n)
            True
        Case Else
            False

Rem Bring a stashed thing into view without disturbing which state it is in.
Def Revealed(g As Game, i As Number) As Game
    Select Case PropView(g, i)
        Case Stashed(n)
            SetPropView(g, i, InState(n))
        Case Else
            g

Rem An object is "here" if you are carrying it or it is
Rem lying in this room.
Def Here(g As Game, i As Number) As Boolean
    Toting(g, i) Or Contains(HereList(g), i)

Rem AT is lying in this room; HERE also counts what you
Rem are carrying. The grate is AT you from either side of it.
Def At(g As Game, i As Number) As Boolean
    Contains(HereList(g), i)

Rem Dark unless the room lights itself or you have a
Rem burning lamp with you.
Def IsDarkHere(g As Game) As Boolean
    (Not Contains(Conds(RoomAt(g)), "LIT")
     And (PropOf(g, Lamp()) = LampDark() Or Not Here(g, Lamp())))

Rem Setting an object's state announces the change; only
Rem some objects have anything to say about it.
Def StateChange(g As Game, i As Number, st As Number) As Game
    Let o = ObjAt(i)
    If st >= 0 And st < Length(Changes(o)) Then
        With(Voiced(g, Nth(Changes(o), st + 1)), Props = SetNth(Props(g), i + 1, st))
    Else
        With(g, Props = SetNth(Props(g), i + 1, st))

